messages:
priya_sales: Hey team — ACME Bank wants a week-long POC to validate streaming webhooks + function-calls. Key asks: high-throughput (5k QPS peak), HMAC handshake for webhooks, structured JSON output for downstream systems, and answers to a security questionnaire by EOD Wed. They also asked about Reserved/Dedicated pricing for predictable latency. Can engineering help scope blockers and an ETA?

alex_se: Thanks Priya. Couple quick q's: is the webhook uni-directional (they push events) or do we need to call back into their tools (mutual calls)? Also do they require mTLS or just HMAC?

priya_sales: Primarily uni-directional pushes from Redwood -> their ingestion endpoint, but they want a test harness where our SDK will call an internal function that then POSTS to their sink for validation. They prefer HMAC but said they can do mTLS if we recommend it for banking.

marcus_se: Okay. For 5k QPS sustained we need to confirm whether this is across a single model route or many. Also streaming + function-calls means we should use a streaming socket that proxies token chunks and emits 'invoke' events to their function endpoint. We can demo with our hosted API using streaming websocket and a small adapter.

nina_security: Heads up — ACME's infosec will want: network flow logs, KMS integration, retention policies, and a contract clause about data-at-rest encryption. Also we will need to fill out their questionnaire — send me the doc. We should not advise mTLS by default unless ops can provision certs in their env. HMAC with rotating keys is acceptable for the POC.

priya_sales: Attached their questionnaire link: https://acme.example/qna (redacted). They flagged: data residency (EU), SSO for console access, and SOC2 compliance proof. They asked about token persistence and logs.

alex_se: For the technical demo I can build a small Node SDK sample that: 1) opens a websocket to our streaming endpoint, 2) listens for \u201cfunction_call\u201d events, 3) POSTs to a local test webhook with an HMAC header, 4) streams back structured JSON result. Example snippet we can use in the runbook:

```
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://api.redwood.ai/stream?model=rw-instruct');
ws.on('message', async (m) => {
  const evt = JSON.parse(m);
  if (evt.type === 'function_call') {
    const res = await fetch('https://localhook.test/recv', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'x-acme-hmac': sign(evt.payload, KEY) },
      body: JSON.stringify(evt.payload)
    });
    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ id: evt.id, result: await res.text() }));
  }
});
```

marcus_se: That looks good. For throughput: if they want dedicated latency SLA we should propose Redwood Dedicated with a reserved pool sized for 5k QPS. Rough estimate: for short responses (<100 tokens) on A100-class nodes, that might map to ~4-6 gpus per 1k QPS depending on batching. I'll prepare a cost-ballpark for Dedicated vs hosted bursts.

priya_sales: Can you also confirm fallback behavior? They want graceful degradation if capacity is hit.

alex_se: We can configure smart routing: primary -> Dedicated pool, fallback -> hosted multimodel with a slightly faster but more quantized variant. We'll document expected latency change and why outputs may vary slightly when quantized.

nina_security: For the questionnaire answers: I'll provide standard SOC2/Security boilerplate. But need input on: do we log raw prompts or only token-level aggregates? And what's the retention period for PII? We should default to 30 days for POC and say we can turn on data deletion hooks.

priya_sales: ACME insists on no raw prompt retention beyond 7 days in POC. Can we accommodate?

marcus_se: We can set a POC policy: redact PII client-side (recommended) + server-side retention at 7 days. Will need ops to set retention flag for this org_id. Ops: is that quick to change for POC?

deploy-bot: deploy #config-change for org=acme-poc scheduled by ops at 2026-04-10T18:02:00Z (eta ~ 4h) :white_check_mark:

priya_sales: Great — timeline: need security answers by Wed EOD, demo-ready SDK + runbook by Thu AM, and cost estimate for Dedicated vs hosted by Thu EOD. Any blockers?

alex_se: I have a local sample ready, will push a slim runbook repo link tonight. Need a test webhook URL from ACME (or we can use a temporary ngrok for demo).

nina_security: I'll draft the questionnaire answers and flag any legal exceptions. If they require mTLS, legal+ops needs lead time for certs. HMAC + rotating key is the fastest path.

marcus_se: I'll produce the capacity/cost estimate and a short doc explaining structured output schema enforcement (we'll use JSON Schema + strict validation to avoid downstream parsing errors). Also will include notes about function-call idempotency and retries for under-load.

priya_sales: Perfect. I'll reach out to ACME for the webhook endpoint and share the questionnaire PDF with Nina. Thanks all — keep updates in this thread. :v
